Now produce the full final design as stage=result.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-4f8c89afafe74759b8a0a9f156f4919a", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-4f8c89afafe74759b8a0a9f156f4919a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Experiment Design Document — Causal Discovery with Unobserved Common Causes via CAMUV\n\n**Design-only deliverable. No experiment was run; all \"would be / expected\" statements are planning content and are explicitly not claims of execution.**\n\n## 0. Task frame (fixed requirements)\nAnswer the question: *\"How can you discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes using the CAMUV algorithm?\"* by specifying a controlled experiment that (a) generates ANM data under latent confounders, (b) applies a CAMUV-style algorithm, (c) compares it against suitable baselines, and (d) quantifies discovery quality under defined metrics and an analysis plan. Environment: /workspace contains only task.json; network disabled; no ML/causal packages importable (verified: numpy/scipy/sklearn/networkx all raise ModuleNotFoundError).\n\n---\n\n## 1. CAMUV decision rule — explicit labelled assumption (point 1, locked)\n\nThe published paper (Sharma, Ravikumar & Zhang, \"Causal Discovery using Additive Noise Models with Unobserved Variables\") could **not** be verified against the actual text in this environment (no source material, network off). The following is therefore stated as our **reconstruction**, a working assumption the whole experiment is defined against — it must be treated in all interpretation as \"CAMUV-as-reconstructed\", not as a confirmed theorem. Its impact on evaluation is noted in §6 (metrics) and §9 (failure modes).\n\n**Model class assumed (CAMUV DGP).** Each observed variable is an additive noise model over its direct causes, which may be observed or latent: X_j = f