UV algorithm?\"* by specifying a controlled experiment that (a) generates ANM data under latent confounders, (b) applies a CAMUV-style algorithm, (c) compares it against suitable baselines, and (d) quantifies discovery quality under defined metrics and an analysis plan. Environment: /workspace contains only task.json; network disabled; no ML/causal packages importable (verified: numpy/scipy/sklearn/networkx all raise ModuleNotFoundError).\n\n---\n\n## 1. CAMUV decision rule \u2014 explicit labelled assumption (point 1, locked)\n\nThe published paper (Sharma, Ravikumar & Zhang, \"Causal Discovery using Additive Noise Models with Unobserved Variables\") could **not** be verified against the actual text in this environment (no source material, network off). The following is therefore stated as our **reconstruction**, a working assumption the whole experiment is defined against \u2014 it must be treated in all interpretation as \"CAMUV-as-reconstructed\", not as a confirmed theorem. Its impact on evaluation is noted in \u00a76 (metrics) and \u00a77 (failure modes).\n\n**Model class assumed (CAMUV DGP).** Each observed variable is an additive noise model over its direct causes, which may be observed or latent: X_j = f_j(Pa_j) + N_j, with N_j independent of Pa_j. The underlying causal graph over O \u222a L (observed O, latent L) is a DAG; faithfulness and generic ANM-identifiability conditions are assumed to hold in the simulated data (they hold by construction for most of our generator settings).\n\n**Pairwise test.** For each ordered pair (i, j): fit f\u0302 via nonparametric regression (Gaussian Process with RBF kernel for n \u2264 3000, random forest for larger n), form residual R_{j|i} = X_j \u2212 f\u0302_j(X_i), and test independence of X_i and R_{j|i} with a kernel-based HSIC test (permutation p-value, n_perm \u2248 300, or Gamma-approximation asymptotic p-value for speed). \"X_i explains X_j\" \u21d4 p_{i\u2192j} > \u03b1 (independence accepted), with \u03b1 family-wise controlled via Benjamini\u2013Hochberg over all pairs.\n\n**Decision rule (reconstruction).** For each unordered observed pair {i, j}:\n1. **Exactly one direction independent** (p_{i\u2192j} > \u03b1, p_{j\u2192i} \u2264 \u03b1, or vice-versa) \u2192 infer **directed edge** i\u2192j (the independent direction).\n2. **Both directions dependent** (p_{i\u2192j} \u2264 \u03b1 AND p_{j\u2192i} \u2264 \u03b1) \u2192 infer **latent common cause** present, no direct observed edge.\n3. **Both directions independent** \u2192 **ambiguous**: output no edge, record in an explicit \"ambiguity\" bucket.\n\n**Why this rule answers the question.** In a pure ANM (no latents), identifiability relies on the asymmetry of the residual-independence test. Under latent confounders the key additional fact this rule exploits is that a *confounded* pair with no direct edge is expected to fail *both* directions (the latent leaks into both residuals), whereas a genuine direct edge is expected to pass in its own direction absent a simultaneous confounder on the same tail. The pair whose both directions fail is exactly the \"unobserved common cause\" signature this experiment detects.\n\n**Impact of the labelling on evaluation.** Because rule 3 is our choice, the \"ambiguity rate\" is reported as a first-class metric (\u00a76) and the both-fail\u21d2latent interpretation is validated *empirically* only against confounded ground-truth pairs (S1/S3/S5), not asserted. All CAMUV results are contextualized as \"CAMUV (reconstruction)\".\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data generation (point 3 \u2014 full detail)\n\n**Generator.** Generic ANM engine: input ground-truth DAG over V = O \u222a L; each node j gets f_j (family + parameters, seed-drawn) and noise N_j. Sample X_j = f_j(Pa_j) + N_j in topological order.\n\n**Function families.** linear (a\u00b7x), sine (a\u00b7sin(b\u00b7x+\u03c6)), quadratic (a\u00b7x\u00b2+b\u00b7x with centering), and cubic (a\u00b7x\u00b3 + b\u00b7x). Per edge, family drawn uniformly from the non-linear ones (linear only in dedicated ablation). Edge coefficients a \u2208 {0.5, 1.0, 1.5}.\n\n**Noise.** Gaussian \u03c3 \u2208 {0.1, 0.5, 1.0} (relative to signal scale), and Uniform(\u2212u, u) with matching scale (in the uniform ablation).\n\n**Latent strength** (scale factor on all latent\u2192observed edge coefficients): weak 0.3 / moderate 1.0 / strong 2.0.\n\n**Sample sizes** n \u2208 {500, 2000, 10000}. **Repetitions**: 20\u201350 fixed seeds (e.g., seeds 0..49), identical seed stream across methods so all comparisons are paired.\n\n**Scenarios (ground-truth graphs over observed + latent nodes).**\n- **S1 \u2014 pure confounder (the core case):** 2 observed X, Y; 1 latent H with H\u2192X, H\u2192Y; no direct edge between X, Y.\n- **S2 \u2014 triangle (no latents):** X\u2192Y, Y\u2192Z, X\u2192Z (3 observed) \u2014 sanity/control that the method still works with no latents.\n- **S3 \u2014 confounded chain:** X\u2192Y\u2192Z (3 observed) plus latent H\u2192Y and H\u2192Z (a hidden confounder in the chain head). Mixes direct edges (X\u2192Y, Y\u2192Z) with a confounded pair (Y,Z).\n- **S4 \u2014 direct edge + confounder on the same pair:** H\u2192X, H\u2192Y, and X\u2192Y. Expected hard case for the pairwise rule (see \u00a77, failure mode 1).\n- **S5 \u2014 scaling:** 4\u20136 observed + 1\u20132 latent; random DAG (Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi, bounded fan-in, ~1.5\u00d7 expected edges) with a random latent confounder attached to 2\u20133 observed nodes. ~30 random graphs \u00d7 seeds.\n\n**Data splits:** each generated dataset is used by every method (no independent re-fitting of the generator), keeping method comparisons paired.\n\n---\n\n## 3. CAMUV implementation spec (point 2 \u2014 libraries & steps)\n\nNo CAMUV/causal library is available or verifiable (workspace empty, network off, no `lingam`/`tigramite`/`dowhy`, and even numpy/scipy/sklearn/networkx are not importable now \u2014 see \u00a79). Therefore the design specifies a **faithful re-implementation** of the method in \u00a71.\n\n- **Dependencies (if this were executed):** numpy, scipy, scikit-learn (`GaussianProcessRegressor` with RBF kernel; `RandomForestRegressor` fallback for n > 3000 to avoid O(n\u00b3) GP cost), networkx (graph bookkeeping, SHD computation). HSIC: custom implementation (standard kernel-based estimator HSIC = trace(K\u00b7H\u00b7L\u00b7H)/m\u00b2 on standardized variables with RBF kernels; p-value via 300 permutations or via the Gamma-moment approximation for the independence asymptotic).\n- **Pipeline steps:** (1) standardize each observed variable; (2) for all ordered pairs, regression \u2192 residual \u2192 HSIC p-value; (3) BH multiple-testing correction across pairs; (4) apply \u00a71 decision rule; (5) write out directed graph over observed nodes + the latent/ambiguous bookkeeping; (6) save per-pair p-values to disk for post-hoc \u03b1-sweep analysis (optional ROC-style trade-off).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Baselines / controls and why (point 3)\n\n1. **Pairwise ANM (no latent handling)** \u2014 the *critical control*: identical regression+HSIC machinery, but interprets \"both directions fail\" as \"no relationship\" and \"both pass\" as \"unrelated\" (never \"latent\"). Isolates the exact contribution the CAMUV rule adds; expected to (wrongly) leave confounded S1 pairs unconnected.\n2. **PC (constraint-based, assumes no latents)** \u2014 standard non-ANM baseline; known to mis-handle latent confounders (over-connect / mis-orient), giving a reference point for the state-of-the-art without latent machinery.\n3. **FCI (constraint-based, explicitly handles latents)** \u2014 the *principled competitor* for unobserved common causes; the fairest head-to-head. Output is a PAG, so \u00a75's projection rule is mandatory.\n4. **Correlation-threshold (|Pearson| > \u03c4)** \u2014 low-bar sanity baseline.\nAll baselines restricted to the same observed-variable subspace as CAMUV.\n\n---\n\n## 5. FCI PAG \u2192 comparable metric projection rule (point 2 \u2014 concrete)\n\nFCI returns a PAG over observed nodes with marks \u2192, o\u2192, o\u2013o, \u2194. We define a deterministic projection so SHD/orientation/precision-recall are comparable to the DAG-like output of CAMUV/ANM:\n\n- **Adjacency projection (level L1):** a pair is \"edge-present\" in the FCI output if the mark is any of o\u2013o, o\u2192, \u2194, \u2192. (All are some kind of adjacency; \u2194 explicitly encodes a latent common cause.)\n- **Direction projection (level L2):** only an unambiguously directed mark **\u2192** counts as an oriented directed edge for direction metrics. **o\u2192** counts as adjacency but is *not* oriented (its head mark is unresolved); **\u2194** counts as a **latent-common-cause annotation** (feeds the confounder-detection metric, not the directed-edge metric).\n- **Orientation-accuracy restriction:** orientation accuracy is computed only over pairs that are edge-adjacent *and* whose ground truth has a directed edge; unresolved o\u2192 marks are counted as \"not oriented\" (neither correct nor wrong, but tracked separately to be fair to FCI).\n- **SHD:** computed on (a) adjacency sets (additions/deletions) via L1, plus (b) a separate orientation-error count via L2 (reversals and directed-vs-undirected mismatches). This two-level SHD decomposition is reported for *all* methods so the comparison is apples-to-apples: CAMUV/ANM also contribute L1(adjacency) and L2(orientation) components.\n- The rule is fixed *before* running (registered in the design), so it can't be tuned post-hoc on results.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Evaluation metrics (point 3)\n\nAll computed on the **observed-variable subgraph only** (latent structure is ground truth for generation, but we evaluate discovery of direct edges among observed variables; latent nodes never appear in metrics, only latent-*detection*).\n- **SHD (adjacency level + orientation level, per \u00a75):** total edge additions + deletions + reversals vs. ground-truth observed subgraph; report the three components separately.\n- **Orientation accuracy:** fraction of inferred directed edges (among observed pairs) whose head direction matches ground truth, restricted to pairs with a ground-truth direct edge.\n- **Edge precision / recall / F1:** on direct-edge presence between observed pairs (ground-truth adjacency restricted to observed subgraph).\n- **Confounder-detection accuracy:** for pairs whose ground truth is \"latent common cause, no direct edge\" (S1, S3 Y\u2013Z, S5 confounded pairs): fraction correctly left unconnected (CAMUV/ANM) or annotated with \u2194 (FCI). This is the metric most directly answering *\"data with unobserved common causes\"*.\n- **Ambiguity rate (CAMUV only):** fraction of observed pairs landing in rule-3 \"both independent\" bucket \u2014 reported explicitly because it is a consequence of our labelled reconstruction (\u00a71).\n- Baseline-comparison sanity: for S2 (no latents) all methods should perform well; degradation on S2 would signal a generator/metric bug.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis plan (point 3)\n\n- **Per-scenario summary:** for each scenario \u00d7 configuration (function family, noise \u03c3/distribution, latent strength, n): mean \u00b1 std (and median \u00b1 IQR) over the 20\u201350 seeds for every metric in \u00a76.\n- **Ablations:** (i) non-linear vs linear functions (ANM identifiability expectation: linear-Gaussian is non-identifiable \u2192 ambiguous bucket grows; document); (ii) Gaussian vs uniform noise; (iii) latent strength weak/moderate/strong (expectation: weak confounding \u2192 near-ANM behavior, strong \u2192 both-fail signature dominates); (iv) sample size 500/2000/10000 (power of the independence test); (v) scale S5.\n- **Significance / pairwise tests:** because seeds are identical across methods, all comparisons are paired: for binary edge indicators use **McNemar's test** (CAMUV vs each baseline per pair, with BH correction); for continuous SHD differences use bootstrap 95% CIs on the paired difference.\n- **Failure-mode section** (expected findings to report honestly, not hidden):\n  1. **S4 (direct edge X\u2192Y + latent confounding on the same tail):** residual of Y-on-X is not independent (the latent leaks into the residual) \u2192 the pairwise rule may report \"both fail\" \u2192 false \"latent-only\" conclusion. Expect lower recall for CAMUV in S4; report as documented limitation.\n  2. **Linear-Gaussian data:** both directions often independent \u2192 ambiguity bucket rises; accuracy falls. Report.\n  3. **Weak direct effect vs strong confounder:** direction may flip toward \"latent-only\".\n  4. **Both-independent ambiguity:** reported, never silently dropped (rule 3 is ours).\n  5. **FCI cost/coverage:** at p > 6 FCI's conditioning-set growth may force early stop; restrict FCI to p \u2264 6 and cap conditioning set size (documented as an FCI-budget caveat).\n- **Deliverables shape:** per-scenario metric table; ablation grids; pairwise significance table; explicit failure-mode write-up; \u03b1-sweep curve (optional, from saved p-values) trading edge recall vs precision.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Rationale summary (why this design answers the question)\n- The both-fail\u21d2latent signature is the *definitional* phenomenon of \"data with unobserved common causes\" under the ANM lens, so making S1 the headline scenario directly tests the algorithm's stated purpose.\n- Pairing CAMUV against (a) its own machinery minus latent handling (ANM) and (b) the established latent-handling method (FCI) isolates the CAMUV rule's unique contribution and locates CAMUV relative to constraint-based methods.\n- The fixed PAG projection (\u00a75) and two-level SHD make cross-method metrics meaningful instead of ad hoc.\n- Explicit labelling of the \u00a71 rule as a reconstruction keeps claims honest given the offline environment.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Feasibility (design-only, honest) (point 4)\n- **No experiment was run**; everything below is a planning specification, no